<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="WP-07">
    <title>Week 7 Weekly Practice</title>
   <page>

    <p>
        Due the first class of the week of October 19.
    </p>

   

    <exercise>
  <title>Exercise 4.4 (i)</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Prove by induction that for all natural numbers <m>n</m>,
      <md>4^0 + 4^1 + 4^2 + 4^3 + \cdots + 4^n = \frac{4^{n+1} - 1}{3}.</md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<theorem>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For all natural numbers <m>n</m>,
      <md>4^0 + 4^1 + 4^2 + 4^3 + \cdots + 4^n = \frac{4^{n+1} - 1}{3}.</md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
    We will use a proof by induction. For each <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m> let <m>S_n</m> be the statement
    <md>4^0 + 4^1 + 4^2 + 4^3 + \cdots + 4^n = \frac{4^{n+1} - 1}{3}.</md>
  </p>
  <p>
    For the base case, consider <m>n=1</m>. Since the left side of <m>S_1</m> is <m>4^0+4^1=1+4=5</m> 
    and the right side is <m>\frac{4^{1+1}-1}{3}= \frac{15}{3}=5</m>, the base case is true.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the inductive step, let <m>k\in\mathbb{N}</m> and assume <m>S_k</m> is true. That is, assume that
      <md>4^0 + 4^1 + 4^2 + 4^3 + \cdots + 4^k = \frac{4^{k+1} - 1}{3}.</md>
    We will prove
    that <m>S_{k+1}</m> is true, or that
    <md>4^0 + 4^1 + 4^2 + 4^3 + \cdots + 4^{k+1} = \frac{4^{(k+1)+1} - 1}{3}.</md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Adding <m>4^{k+1}</m> to both sides of <m> S_k</m> and doing algebra, we obtain
    <md>
      <mrow>4^0 + 4^1 + 4^2 + 4^3 + \cdots +4^k + 4^{k+1} \amp = \frac{4^{k+1} - 1}{3} + 4^{k+1} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \frac{4^{k+1}-1}{3} + \frac{3(4^{k+1})}{3} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \frac{4^{k+1}+3(4^{k+1}-1)}{3} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \frac{4(4^{k+1})-1}{3}</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \frac{4^{(k+1)+1} -1}{3}.</mrow>
    </md>
   Thus, we have shown <m>S_{k+1}</m> and the induction step is complete. 
  </p>

  <p>
    By the Principle of Mathematical Induction, the theorem is true.
  </p>
</proof>

</page>
<page>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-4-2">
  <title>Exercise 4.2</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Provide three proofs that if <m>n</m> is a natural number, then
      <m>n^2 - n</m> is even.
    </p>
    <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove it by cases, by considering <q><m>n</m> is even</q> and
          <q><m>n</m> is odd</q>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove it by applying Proposition 4.2 to the sum
          <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \cdots + (n-1)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove it by induction.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
</p>
   
      <p>
        <em>Note:</em>
        Proposition 4.2 says
        <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \cdots + n = \dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.
      </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<theorem >
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>n</m> is a natural number, then
      <m>n^2 - n</m> is even.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>
<proof>
  <p>
    We will use a proof by cases. Consider the cases where <m>n</m> is even and the case where
    <m>n</m> is odd.
  </p>

  <p>
    <term>Case 1:</term> Suppose <m>n</m> is even. Then <m>n = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.
    By substition and algebra,
    <md> 
      <mrow>n^2 - n \amp (2k)^2 - 2k</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp 4k^2 - 2k </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp 2(2k^2 - k). </mrow>
    </md>
    Since the integers are closed under addition and multiplication, <m>2k^2-k\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
    and so <m>n^2-n</m> is even whenever <m>n</m> is even.
  </p>

  <p>
     <term>Case 2:</term> Suppose <m>n</m> is odd. Then <m>n = 2k+1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.
    By substition and algebra,
    <md> 
      <mrow>n^2 - n \amp (2k+1)^2 - (2k+1)</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = 4k^2 +4k +1 - 2k -1 </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = 4k^2 + 2k  </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp 2(2k^2 + k).</mrow>
    </md>
    Since the integers are closed under addition and multiplication, <m>2k^2+k\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
    and so <m>n^2-n</m> is even whenever <m>n</m> is odd.
  </p>
</proof>
</page>
<page>

<lemma xml:id="lem-trianglenumber">
  <statement>
    <p>
      For all <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m>,
      <md>
        1+2+3 + \dots + n = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}.
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </statement>
</lemma>
<theorem >
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>n</m> is a natural number, then
      <m>n^2 - n</m> is even.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
    Let <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>. Then, by <xref ref="lem-trianglenumber"/>, applied to the sum
    of the first <m>n-1</m> natural numbers, we have that
    <md>
      1+2+\dots + (n-1) = \frac{(n-1)((n-1)+1)}{2}.
    </md>
    By algebra,
    <md>
      2(1+2+\dots + (n-1)) = (n-1)(n).
    </md>
    Since the integers are closed under addition, <m>1+2 + \cdots + (n-1)\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and thus, 
    <m>(n-1)n = n^2-n</m>
    is even.
  </p>
</proof>

<theorem >
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>n</m> is a natural number, then
      <m>n^2 - n</m> is even.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>
<proof>
  <p>
    We will use a proof by induction. For each natural number <m>n</m> define <m>S_n</m> to be
    that <m>n^2-n</m> is even.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the base case, we consider <m>S_1</m>, or when <m>n=1</m>. When <m>n=1</m>, <m>n^2-n = 1^2-1 = 0</m>. 
    Note <m>0</m> is even as <m>0=2(0)</m> and <m>0\in\mathbb{Z}</m>. Thus the theorem is true when <m>n=1</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the inductive step, let <m>k\in\mathbb{N}</m> and assume <m>S_k</m> is true. That is,
    assume that <m> k^2-k </m> is even. We will prove that <m>(k+1)^2 - (k+1)</m> is even. 
    Since <m>k^2-k</m> is even, we know that there exists an integer <m>j</m> such that <m>k^2-k = 2j</m>.
    By algebra and substitution,
    <md>
      <mrow>(k+1)^2-(k+1) \amp =  k^2 + 2k + 1 -k - 1</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = (k^2 - k) + 2k</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = 2j + 2k</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = 2(j+k).</mrow>
    </md>
    Since <m>k</m> and <m>j</m> are integers and the integers are closed under addition, <m>k+j \in\mathbb{Z}</m>.
    Thus, <m>(k+1)^2 - (k+1)</m> is even and the inductive step is complete.
  </p>

  <p>
    By the principle of mathematical induction, <m>n^2 - n</m> is even for all <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>.
  </p>
</proof>





</page>


